#!/usr/bin/env bash
# init_eo -- start a tool: give it a README that says what it is for.
#
# Run it in the new repository. It is the first of three and the only one with
# nothing to comply with:
#
#   init_eo new                    # name it, and write the README
#   init_eo from-child <path>      # the same, for work that already exists
#   join_eo                        # later, when there is something to join with
#   check_join_eo <id>             # run from anoieu, to see where it stands
#
# **Two ways a tool starts here, and they are not the same job.** The mode is
# required rather than defaulted, because the wrong one produces a confident
# README about the wrong thing and nothing downstream catches it:
#
#   new           a repository with nothing in it. The name is in the register,
#                 the scope is a line, and everything else is ahead of it.
#
#   from-child    the work exists already, as `tools/<name>/` in some other
#                 repository in this ecosystem, and a person has decided it
#                 graduates. There is a charter, an account, and a record of
#                 what it delivered -- and that record is the reason this
#                 repository exists, so the README is written *from* it rather
#                 than from the register.
#
# `<path>` is the child project's directory on this machine, wherever the parent
# is checked out. It is read and never written.
#
#   init_eo new --show-prompt
#   init_eo from-child ../anoieu/tools/x --show-prompt
#
# Nothing here is required of the repository and nothing is checked. A new tool
# with a clear purpose and no policy is worth more than a compliant one with
# nothing to say, and the order matters: knowing what you are building is what
# makes the rest of it decidable later.

set -euo pipefail

AGENT=claude
PRINT=0
SHOW=0

usage() {
  cat >&2 <<'USAGE'
usage: init_eo new                 [--codex] [--print] [--show-prompt]
       init_eo from-child <path>   [--codex] [--print] [--show-prompt]

The two ways a tool starts. Pick the one that is true; there is no default,
because guessing wrong produces a confident README about the wrong thing.

  new                a repository with nothing in it yet. The README is written
                     from the ecosystem's name register and from what you say
                     the tool is for.

  from-child <path>  work that already exists as a child project in another
                     repository here, which a person has decided graduates.
                     <path> is that directory -- read, never written -- and the
                     README is written from its charter and from what it
                     actually delivered.

  --codex         run codex instead of claude
  --print         run non-interactively and print the result
  --show-prompt   print the prompt and exit, running nothing

Run this in the new repository, not in anoieu and not in the parent.
USAGE
  exit 2
}

MODE=
CHILD=

case "${1:-}" in
  new)        MODE=new; shift ;;
  from-child) MODE=from-child; shift
              case "${1:-}" in
                ""|-*) echo "init_eo: from-child needs the child project's directory" >&2; usage ;;
              esac
              CHILD=$1; shift ;;
  -h|--help)  usage ;;
  "")         echo "init_eo: say which of the two this is" >&2; usage ;;
  *)          echo "init_eo: unknown mode $1" >&2; usage ;;
esac

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --codex) AGENT=codex; shift ;;
    --claude) AGENT=claude; shift ;;
    --print) PRINT=1; shift ;;
    --show-prompt) SHOW=1; shift ;;
    -h|--help) usage ;;
    *) echo "init_eo: unknown option $1" >&2; usage ;;
  esac
done

# An absolute path reads the same from wherever the agent ends up. Resolved
# only if it is there: --show-prompt still prints what it would send.
if [ "$MODE" = from-child ] && [ -d "$CHILD" ]; then
  CHILD=$(cd "$CHILD" && pwd)
fi

read -r -d '' OPEN_NEW <<'PROMPT_END' || true
This is a brand new tool in the Eunoia ecosystem: a repository with nothing in
it yet. Write the README that says what it is for. That is the whole of the job.

**The name is already decided.** You do not choose it, and you do not propose an
alternative — it was settled before this repository existed, and it is recorded
in the ecosystem's register:

  https://github.com/ajreynol/anoieu/blob/main/tools/ynoia/names.md

Find the entry whose name matches this repository's. It carries the etymology and
a line of scope, and it may point at a proposal with more. **If there is no
entry, stop and say so** rather than inventing one: a name nobody registered
means a step was skipped, and that is much cheaper to find out now than after a
README argues for it.
PROMPT_END

read -r -d '' OPEN_CHILD <<'PROMPT_END' || true
This tool is not a new idea. The work already exists, as a **child project** in
another repository in this ecosystem, and a person has decided that it graduates
into this one. Write the README that says what it is for. That is the whole of
the job.

**Read it first, all of it:**

  @CHILD@

Its README is a charter: the question it set out to answer, the goals in
order, the stretch goal, and — the part that does the work — an explicit list
of what it would *not* do. Read the rest of the directory too. If it carries a
statement of what it delivered and of which child-project rules stopped being
true of it, read that most carefully of all: **that list is the reason this
repository exists.** A child project graduates because it acquired users, and
that statement is where it said so.

**The name is already taken, and by this work.** It is in the ecosystem's
register, with a line saying where it has been living:

  https://github.com/ajreynol/anoieu/blob/main/tools/ynoia/names.md

Do not re-derive the etymology and do not improve on it. The child's own README
explains the name in a sentence somebody can disagree with; that sentence comes
across, and the register is what you check it against. **If the register has no
entry for it, stop and say so** rather than inventing one.

**This README is not the child's README moved.** The old front page was written
to say the work was speculative, unadvertised and depended on by nobody, because
that is what a child project has to say about itself. Where those things have
stopped being true, this one says so plainly; where they are still true, it says
that instead. **Claim nothing the child did not actually deliver** — a
repository that opens by describing what it is going to become has taken the one
thing graduating was supposed to prove and turned it back into a promise.
PROMPT_END

read -r -d '' BODY <<'PROMPT_END' || true
**The README.** One screen, and a reader who has never heard of any of this
should finish it knowing:

  - what the tool is, and what question it answers;
  - the question it does *not* answer, which is usually the larger one somebody
    will assume;
  - what it would take to run it, even if that is "nothing works yet";
  - the name, and why that word — taken from what is written down already
    rather than invented here. You may say where you think the etymology is a
    stretch; the name stands either way.

Write what it is *for*, not what it will contain. A roadmap read as a promise is
worse than no roadmap, so where something does not exist, say so plainly in the
present tense.

**Comply with nothing.** There is a shared repository policy, and this is not
the moment for it: no maintenance note, no discussion file, no CI, no layout, no
documentation index. `join_eo` does that later, when there is something to join
with, and it will tell you exactly what to add. Do not add files in
anticipation, and do not read the policy now — knowing what you are building is
what makes the rest of it decidable, and that order is deliberate.
PROMPT_END

read -r -d '' TAIL_NEW <<'PROMPT_END' || true
**Leave a record of what you read.** Copy, verbatim, the register entry for the
name you took and the proposal section it points to if there is one, into
`ynoia-brief.local.md` at the root. Do not stage it: `*.local.md` is the
ecosystem's suffix for a file that is deliberately not committed. It is there so
that if this README turns out to be wrong, somebody can see what you were working
from instead of guessing — the register moves, and the version you read is the
only thing that explains what you wrote.
PROMPT_END

read -r -d '' TAIL_CHILD <<'PROMPT_END' || true
**Leave a record of what you read.** Copy, verbatim, the child project's charter
and its statement of what it delivered, into `ynoia-brief.local.md` at the root,
naming the path and the parent's commit you read them at. Do not stage it:
`*.local.md` is the ecosystem's suffix for a file that is deliberately not
committed. The parent keeps moving and the child's directory may be retired in
it, so the version you read is the only thing that explains what you wrote.

**The parent's tree is not yours to change.** The child's directory stays where
it is until a person retires it there, and how much of it comes across — the
account, the ledger, the history — is a person's decision rather than yours.
Copy nothing on your own initiative except the README you are writing.

**Two things are owed elsewhere, and neither of them is yours to do.** Say so
at the end instead. The register entry for the name has to say where it lives
now rather than where it lived. And any role the child held in anoieu's
`docs/roles.md` moves under this repository's own heading and **keeps its id**
— a role that changes hands keeps its number, which is what makes every
decision recorded against it survive the move.
PROMPT_END

read -r -d '' CLOSE <<'PROMPT_END' || true
Leave the work staged and not committed. Then say in two sentences what you took
the scope to be, so a person can correct it before anything is built on it.
PROMPT_END

# Joined explicitly: `read` strips a heredoc's leading newline, so a blank line
# between two sections has to be put back here rather than assumed.
NL=$'\n\n'
if [ "$MODE" = new ]; then
  PROMPT="$OPEN_NEW$NL$BODY$NL$TAIL_NEW$NL$CLOSE"
else
  PROMPT="${OPEN_CHILD//@CHILD@/$CHILD}$NL$BODY$NL$TAIL_CHILD$NL$CLOSE"
fi

if [ "$SHOW" = 1 ]; then printf '%s\n' "$PROMPT"; exit 0; fi

# Refuse a typo rather than writing a README from nothing -- the same rule
# welcome_eo keeps. A directory with no README carries no charter, so there is
# nothing here that `init_eo new` would not do better.
if [ "$MODE" = from-child ]; then
  [ -d "$CHILD" ] || { echo "init_eo: $CHILD is not a directory" >&2; exit 2; }
  [ -f "$CHILD/README.md" ] || {
    echo "init_eo: $CHILD has no README.md, so there is no charter to write from." >&2
    echo "         If the work has no stated scope yet, this is 'init_eo new'." >&2
    exit 2; }
fi

command -v "$AGENT" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  echo "init_eo: $AGENT is not on PATH" >&2; exit 127; }

ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || pwd)
echo "-- init_eo $MODE: $(basename "$ROOT") ($ROOT), via $AGENT" >&2
[ "$MODE" = from-child ] && echo "--   from: $CHILD" >&2

cd "$ROOT"
if [ "$AGENT" = codex ]; then
  if [ "$PRINT" = 1 ]; then exec codex exec "$PROMPT"; else exec codex "$PROMPT"; fi
else
  if [ "$PRINT" = 1 ]; then exec claude -p "$PROMPT"; else exec claude "$PROMPT"; fi
fi
